description:
Follow-on to rf2-kqxe6.5 (PR #6875), which landed the routing-side R1: fire-and-forget :on-match, the pure routing-side readiness projector (re-frame.routing.readiness/project-at-commit), and the retirement of the on-match error machinery + route :on-error. OUT of the routing worker's implementation/routing/** surface, so deferred:

Unify the resource-settle / hydration / epoch-restore readiness reconciliation onto ONE pure projector bound to Spec 016 vocabulary (Spec 012 §Route readiness is a resource projection, EP-0037 OI-2):
- read :rf.resource/has-data? / loading? AT COMMIT so an already-fresh blocking resource projects :idle immediately (no transient :loading) — currently re-frame.resources.route/route-resource-plan reports blocking scoped-keys and commit-navigation seeds :loading unconditionally when blocking is present;
- distinguish a background-refresh failure (:fetching with usable data → stays on :refresh-error, route NOT :error) from a blocking first-load failure precisely in re-frame.resources.route/drain-blocking (currently keys off :status = :error);
- previous-data (:keep-previous?) projection must NOT complete a newly-keyed requirement's first load;
- reconcile the cached :transition/:error through the projector on HYDRATION (implementation/ssr) and EPOCH RESTORE (implementation/core epoch) — hydration/restore must not preserve a :loading/:error the restored resource state contradicts.

Surfaces: implementation/resources/** (drain-blocking, route.cljc, ssr.cljc), implementation/core/** (epoch), implementation/ssr/** (hydrate). The routing-side projector + on-route-entry plan seam are already in place to build on. Also: re-frame.resources.route still publishes the now-unconsumed :routing/route-blocking? late-bind hook (settle-transition was retired) — drop it. And the stale settle-transition doc-strings in implementation/core/late_bind/directory.cljc + resources/route.cljc.

Ratified from the 2026-07-20 UIx adversarial reviews, Mike-approved. All decisions here are final; no operator input is required to implement.
AUTHORITY: parent epic rf2-u53yy carries the programme's shared reject fence and sequencing rules and is the in-beads authority; this bead restates its own scope fully and is implementable standalone.

THE PROBLEM (verified refs): every consumer needs BOTH top-level entries in shadow-cljs.edn (docs/core/how-to/install-re-frame-ui.md:34-35, the rf2:shadow-ui-contract block):

  {:cache-blockers #{re-frame.ui}
   :build-defaults {:build-hooks [(re-frame.ui.compiler.build-hook/hook)]}}

Both entries are load-bearing. CAUSE: macro expansion mutates registries that a whole-build hook (implementation/ui/src/re_frame/ui/compiler/build_hook.clj) consumes, so Shadow must re-expand every consuming namespace on every build. This leaks compiler internals into every consumer's build configuration, charges a non-obvious compile-time cost to every app forever, and gives humans and agents two ways to produce a plausible-but-incomplete build. An exact Shadow version is presently part of the contract. This is the substrate's largest product defect as distinct from its grammar defects.

THE WORK: move whole-build truth off macro-expansion side effects — emit/harvest per-namespace descriptors in a form Shadow can cache safely; aggregate them in the build hook.

ACCEPTANCE:
- :cache-blockers #{re-frame.ui} is unnecessary;
- warm builds reuse consumer namespace caches with manifests, hook-signatures, schemas, and diagnostics intact;
- at worst ONE build-hook line remains (ideally dependency-only installation);
- supported Shadow versions become a tested range, not one exact pin;
- warm-cache / cold-build / watch / module / advanced probes remain green;
- docs/core/how-to/install-re-frame-ui.md rewritten to the new (smaller) contract in the same PR;
- implementation/shadow-cljs.edn (HOT-ZONE) updated in the same PR.

FENCE: do NOT hide the two lines behind a scaffold helper — that improves appearance while keeping the real cost and failure mode.

DOCS (same PR): docs/core/how-to/install-re-frame-ui.md is the page of record — rewrite the rf2:shadow-ui-contract block and all surrounding prose explaining the cache blocker; sweep docs/core/re-frame.ui/index.md and interop-and-limits.md for restatements of the two-line contract and update them.
EXAMPLES (same PR): sweep examples/** for shadow-cljs.edn files carrying the old contract (notably the standalone consumer-shaped examples/ui/minimal-counter build) and update them to the new contract; everything still compiles. Examples are teaching surfaces — they model the new install story, not the workaround.
EPs (same PR): docs/EP/EP-0030 wherever it states the build/cache contract — reconcile via a DATED ADDENDUM/ERRATUM recording the old clause and the new disposition (the EP-0009 convention); never silently rewrite accepted EP text.

PUBLICATION GATE: this bead BLOCKS rf2-vxgfnd.99.2 (day8/re-frame2-ui publication; dependency edge wired) — an experimental checkout may carry the cache-blocker tax; a published library may not.

WORKER RULES: run the worker worktree guard from the intended checkout BEFORE any edit — sh scripts/assert-worker-worktree.sh (POSIX) or powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts/assert-worker-worktree.ps1 (Windows) — and report the printed WORKTREE_ROOT. Commit first, then git pull --rebase; NEVER git stash (stash use has contaminated worker branches before). Run gates in the FOREGROUND only (backgrounded or tail-piped gates strand work uncommitted); run the focused gates named below, not the full spine (CI owns the spine).

GATES (focused): from implementation/ui: clojure -M:test. From implementation/: npm run test:cljs; npm run test:elision (manifest/harvest surface). The warm-cache / cold-build / watch / module / advanced probes named in acceptance. mkdocs build --strict from repo root (install guide touched).

PR #6277 adds the missing `route-link` and seven `re-frame.ui.react` rows to the human S3 profile, but leaves the executable drift guard unable to verify them. On current main, `profile-catalogues-every-verb-row-exactly` loops only `frozen-s3-verbs`; no document test loops `frozen-s3-view` or `frozen-react-wrappers`. The only remaining profile assertion is a whole-document `"Non-surfaces"` substring.

Consequences:
- deleting the `ui/route-link` table row stays green;
- deleting any React wrapper row stays green;
- swapping route-link or wrapper contracts, direct-call behavior, or proof homes stays green;
- `frozen-react-wrappers` cannot be the claimed source of truth for those details because each entry contains only `:kind` and (for hooks) `:error`, with no contract or JVM/CLJS/HMR proof fields.

This is the exact residual called out by rf2-ar0ga's acceptance: row deletion or swapped proof/contract must turn the test red. The PR body narrowed itself to doc-only, but closing the source Bead did not revise that acceptance, and the profile now explicitly tells programmers/models that all three maps are the source of truth it restates.

Extend the existing bounded `table-row-for` check. Give the one view and seven wrapper descriptors the small exact fragments/proof homes already present in the profile, then assert exactly one matching row and the required fields. Keep runtime behavior in the named proof suites; this gate only binds catalogue association. Do not build a Markdown parser or documentation generator.

## Confirmed current gap

PR #6018 closes `rf2-vxgfnd.142`, but current `rules.cljc` documents that its runtime contract remains incomplete:

- lines 1029-1035 admit that a saved source file deleted with no reloading dependent is never reported by `SHADOW_NS_RESET`, so its custom-element rows persist until a full page load;
- the same gap strands the old namespace on rename;
- lines 779-788 admit that two builds sharing one `$CLJS`, namespace copy, atoms, and `defonce` producer cannot be distinguished.

The “no design can observe an unsaved deletion” ruling concerns a bare unsaved REPL deletion; it does not justify ignoring a **saved file graph delta**. The compile-side Shadow hook already observes authoritative `:build-sources` membership and reconciles deleted/renamed sources, so the runtime needs a narrow successful-build projection/delta into the existing ledger rather than another registry.

## Proof gap

The current tests simulate `before-load-src` by directly calling `notify-reload!`, iterating `SHADOW_NS_RESET`, supplying explicit `:app`/`:lib` arities, and calling `commit-reload!`. They do not run a real Shadow watch or edit/delete/rename sources. Consequently they cannot establish:

- hook discovery/order or annotation load-bearingness;
- actual non-default build identity at the runtime seam;
- two real co-loaded builds;
- removed-source deltas;
- failed evaluation and successful retry.

This is exactly the synthetic-seam limitation the source Bead required the PR to replace.

## Deliverable

Carry stable build identity and the successful build's source membership/delta through one authoritative path into the existing runtime cycle. Prefer reusing the compiler/Shadow build-lifecycle authority and the repository's existing warm-watch harness rather than inventing a second watch protocol.

If Shadow truly cannot support multiple builds sharing one runtime copy, enforce and document that boundary everywhere and remove the explicit-arity tests/claims that present synthetic multi-build isolation as shipped behavior. The preferred pre-alpha result remains real isolation because co-loaded tools/builds are an important power/ergonomics case.

## Provenance and dedupe

Found during the chronological completeness/quality audit of PR #6018. All-status search found only closed `rf2-vxgfnd.67`, `.77`, and `.142`; no live issue owns the remaining real integration.

Found by the rf2-ddu3i probe (PR #7082) while proving this file is NOT a donor-source consumer.

implementation/scripts/check-examples-assets.test.cjs carries:

    it('LIVE: the standalone minimal-counter scaffold host page is NOT enumerated', () => {
      const rel = realIndexes.map(...);
      assert.ok(!rel.some((p) => p.startsWith('ui/minimal-counter/')), ...);
    });

It is a pure NEGATIVE over the real examples/ tree. Delete examples/ui/minimal-counter/ and it passes while asserting nothing: 0 of 0, indistinguishable from a real pass by exit code. The rule it is meant to prove — a standalone project root bearing its own shadow-cljs.edn is pruned from the gallery host-page walk — then has only its synthetic sibling test (which uses a fabricated examples/ui/demo-scaffold path and never touches disk).

This does NOT fire at F6e: examples/ui/minimal-counter/ lives under examples/, not implementation/ui/, and the deletion of the donor tree leaves it alone (measured rc 0 -> 0 with implementation/ui/ moved aside). It fires whenever the scaffold example itself is finally removed — its ledger row (examples/ui/minimal-counter/*, MOVE, F6) is already marked done, so nothing else is watching.

Fix, small: assert the precondition alongside the negative — that examples/ui/minimal-counter/index.html (or its resources/public/ host page) EXISTS and that the walk found it as a candidate before pruning — so the day the scaffold goes, the test says so instead of going quiet.

Found while classifying check-elision.cjs for rf2-ddu3i (PR #7082). Independent of the donor.

check-elision.cjs asserts 60 DEV_ONLY_SENTINELS are ABSENT from the production bundle. An absence assertion is worthless on its own — a sentinel that was renamed, moved out of a gated branch, or whose rooting code was deleted is absent for the wrong reason and passes. The control bundle is the entire methodology: the same 60 must be PRESENT under goog.DEBUG=true.

That check is conditional:

    let control = { ok: true, skipped: true };
    if (fs.existsSync(controlDir)) { control = checkBundle(...); }
    else { report.detail('[elision] control bundle not built — skipping methodology check.'); }

report.detail, not an error; ok stays true; the run exits 0. The PASS line does say 'control skipped', but a green gate whose verdict line admits it proved nothing is the exact blindness family this repo keeps finding.

Today npm run test:elision builds both bundles in one command, so the skip does not fire in CI. That is the whole safety margin, and it is a property of a package.json script rather than of the gate.

Measured population, for the record: DEV_ONLY_SENTINELS 60, of which 8 are re-frame.ui bodies; PROD_SURVIVING 1; PROD_ABSENT_WHEN_UNUSED 2.

Suggested fix, small: make the missing control bundle a FAILURE rather than a skip, matching the sibling non-vacuity floors already in the same file (the 'bundle present but empty' arm at check-elision.cjs:804 already fails closed on exactly this reasoning). If a local run legitimately wants the production arm alone, that is an explicit flag.

PR #6303 correctly replaced Shadow's timestamp-derived `[:shadow.build/build-info :compiled]` set with re-frame.ui's analyzer-pass compile witness, but active implementation and executable-test comments still teach the retired authority.

The production namespace docstring in `compiler/build_hook.clj:55` first says the timestamp set is deliberately not used, then says the later-hook gap is closed by "the finish-time marker and Shadow's compile record". Several live fixtures in `compiler_digest_carrier_jvm_test.clj` repeat the contradiction:

- lines 280-288 call `:cached false` plus a fresh `:js` object "Shadow's causal per-source compile evidence";
- lines 321-323 and 385-387 say the manually driven source is in Shadow's causal `::build-info :compiled` record;
- lines 636-638 say the marker plus that record distinguish a genuine compile;
- lines 777-784 say the current verdict consults Shadow's causal compile record;
- line 809 says `finish` is not told compilation via `::build-info` even though the helper now causally drives the installed analyzer pass and never writes that set.

These are current authority comments, not archaeology: one is the implementation overview and the others sit beside live tests/mutation explanations, exactly where a programmer or coding agent will learn the mechanism. They directly contradict the new production contract and can cause the timestamp authority to be reintroduced. Reconcile this bounded comment cluster only; runtime and tests are already green and correct.

## Problem

PR #5853 closed source bead rf2-vxgfnd.139 after changing the internal installed-plan record and its tool/test projection, but it did not perform the source bead's required normative reconciliation.

The runtime now uses or exposes these concepts in implementation/ui/src/re_frame/ui/frames.cljc:

- an internal monotone :rev guarding a specific record write;
- :committed true as purported root-scope evidence;
- :mount-incomplete for a fresh/uncommitted failed attempt;
- :preflight-attempt-failed for a failed attempt against an already-live/boot-authoritative record;
- finalize/abort clearing rules driven by a preflight receipt;
- installed-plan-entry strips :rev but exposes the other evidence flags.

The tracked contracts still describe the older model:

- spec/004C-Roots-and-Mount.md:405-423 says the installed conflict payload contains the recorded install/adopt record "verbatim" and mentions only optional :mount-incomplete. It does not define :committed, :preflight-attempt-failed, attempt phase, exact identity ownership, or clearing/retry behavior.
- spec/009-Instrumentation.md:2313 catalogues :rf.error/frame-payload-conflict with the old installed/arriving projection and likewise omits the new evidence vocabulary and diagnostic semantics.
- The closed rf2-vxgfnd.139 acceptance criteria explicitly required Spec 004C, Spec 009, runtime record docstrings, tool/test accessors, diagnostics, and tests to use one closed vocabulary. Its close notes acknowledge that the spec follow-up remains.

This is not cosmetic. The current runtime comments call root.render return a host commit and the record model allows a foreign :found-live attempt to settle another root's revision. Those correctness defects are now owned by rf2-vxgfnd.248 and rf2-vxgfnd.191. Writing the current boolean/revision shape into the normative specs before those settle would canonize semantics already shown false.

## Required work

After rf2-vxgfnd.248 and rf2-vxgfnd.191 settle the runtime model, reconcile the normative and projected vocabulary in one focused hot-zone pass:

1. Spec 004C owns compiled-root preflight sequencing and must separately define:
   - frame/boot plan authority;
   - exact root scope and the host evidence that establishes it;
   - fresh/uncommitted versus previously committed attempts;
   - plan-phase versus post-preflight/host-phase failure;
   - record/attempt/incarnation ownership and stale-settlement rejection;
   - retry/finalize/abort clearing behavior;
   - which fields are internal and which may appear in tooling/error payloads.
2. Spec 009's frame-payload-conflict row must describe the same public diagnostic projection and recovery without implying that a live frame or scheduled React update proves a committed root/DOM.
3. installed-plan-entry, frame/client docstrings, conflict message branches, tool/test projection, and fixtures must use exactly the same names and meanings.
4. Resolve the word "verbatim": either the diagnostic really returns the defined external projection, or the projection and internal record are explicitly named as distinct values. Do not expose :rev merely to make the sentence true.

Keep this a documentation/projection reconciliation. Do not add a public API, compatibility layer, second lifecycle ledger, or production-only bookkeeping beyond what the settled runtime correction already needs.

## Sequencing, dedupe, and provenance

This bead is intentionally blocked on rf2-vxgfnd.248 (real React commit acknowledgement) and rf2-vxgfnd.191 (exact authority/equal-plan ownership). Open rf2-vxgfnd.177 only fixes which spec owns compiled-root sequencing; it does not own the attempt-evidence schema. The full current inventory contained no open/in-progress bead for this reconciliation. Two independent exact-head audits of PR #5853 reported the omission.

